---
import Layout from '@boring-sites/design/Layout.astro';
import NoLogsBanner from '../../components/NoLogsBanner.astro';
const siteName = 'What Is My IP';
---
<Layout
  title="What is an ISP?"
  description="A plain-English explanation of ISPs: who routes your internet traffic, and what they can see."
  siteName={siteName}
  jsonLd={{
    type: 'Article',
    data: {
      headline: 'What is an ISP?',
      description: 'A plain-English explanation of ISPs and what they can see.',
      author: { '@type': 'Organization', name: siteName },
    },
  }}
>
  <NoLogsBanner />
  <article class="container-prose py-12">
    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold">What is an ISP?</h1>
    <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-neutral-500">4 min read · Last updated 2026-08-18</p>

    <div class="prose mt-8 dark:prose-invert">
      <p>
        Your <strong>ISP</strong> (Internet Service Provider) is the company that connects your home or office to the internet. In the US, common ones include Comcast, AT&T, Verizon, and T-Mobile. In other countries, the names differ but the role is the same.
      </p>

      <h2>What your ISP does</h2>
      <p>
        Your ISP owns (or rents) the physical infrastructure that carries your internet traffic — fiber, cable, DSL, or cellular. When you load a webpage, your request goes:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>From your device to your router</li>
        <li>From your router to your ISP's network</li>
        <li>Across the ISP's network to the destination (e.g. this website's server)</li>
        <li>Back through the ISP's network to your router to your device</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        The ISP is the middleman for every byte you send or receive.
      </p>

      <h2>What your ISP can see</h2>
      <p>
        Without a VPN, your ISP can see:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Your IP address (they assigned it to you)</li>
        <li>The IP addresses you connect to (every site you visit, every app you use)</li>
        <li>The volume of traffic and timing (when you used the internet, how much)</li>
        <li>DNS queries (which domain names you looked up, unless DNS-over-HTTPS is enabled)</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Your ISP <strong>cannot see</strong>, with HTTPS enabled:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The content of the pages you visit (the URL path, the body of requests, the body of responses — all encrypted)</li>
        <li>What you typed into forms (encrypted)</li>
        <li>Your passwords (encrypted in transit)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What your ISP can do</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Throttle</strong> your connection for certain types of traffic (most ISPs in the US have net-neutrality rules against this, but enforcement varies)</li>
        <li><strong>Block</strong> access to specific sites or services (court-ordered, or in some countries, government-mandated)</li>
        <li><strong>Sell</strong> aggregated browsing data to advertisers (in the US, ISPs have more leeway than websites)</li>
        <li><strong>Hand over</strong> records to law enforcement with a warrant or subpoena</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>What you can do about it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Use HTTPS</strong> for everything (modern browsers warn you when it's not; most sites do)</li>
        <li><strong>Use DNS-over-HTTPS</strong> (DoH) so your ISP can't see your DNS queries</li>
        <li><strong>Use a VPN</strong> to hide your traffic from your ISP (but then the VPN provider can see it instead)</li>
        <li><strong>Use Tor</strong> for stronger anonymity (slow, but no single party can see both your IP and your traffic)</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>How to find your ISP</h2>
      <p>
        Your ISP shows up in the geolocation data attached to your IP. The <a href="/">home page</a> shows your IP; combined with a free geolocation lookup (we don't run one, but there are many), you'd see your ISP's name.
      </p>
    </div>
  </article>
</Layout>
